# PACK FILES ARE BYTES, NOT TEXT. Never let git rewrite one.
#
# catalogue.json publishes a length and a SHA-256 for every pack, and the app
# refuses any download whose bytes do not match: `PackageStore.publishDownloaded`
# throws "That download was corrupted. Try again." and bins the file. So the
# bytes a builder hashes have to be the bytes a rider is served, exactly, for
# ever.
#
# Git's end-of-line conversion breaks that, silently and only on some machines.
# With `core.autocrlf=true` — the Windows default, and what this project's own
# checkout has — a pack git decides is "text" is stored LF and checked out
# CRLF. `tools/build_catalogue.py` hashes the file in the WORKING TREE, so on
# Windows it records the CRLF copy while the LF copy is what gets committed and
# what GitHub Pages serves.
#
# That is not hypothetical: it shipped. `trips/gb.tbtrips` is written by
# `build_trips.py` with `open(path, "w")`, which is enough for git to call it
# text; the published catalogue records 7007 bytes / 4daf8ca3… (the CRLF copy)
# and Pages serves 6764 bytes / e3c05ef5… (the LF copy). Every rider who ever
# tapped "Ready-made trips" got "That download was corrupted", every time, with
# no way to clear it — and nothing anywhere said why.
#
# `-text` turns the conversion off in both directions for these paths. It is
# per-extension rather than a blanket `* -text` so that the Python, YAML and
# Markdown in this repository still normalise the way everybody expects.
*.tbtrips  -text
*.tbpack   -text
# The container formats. Added late, and that is the point: every format above
# was listed the day it started being published and `.tbmap` was not, so the
# rule this whole file exists to state did not cover the thing the app now
# downloads. It survives today only because SQLite writes a NUL at byte 15 and
# git's binary heuristic happens to catch it - which is luck, not a rule, and
# the trips incident below is what luck running out looks like.
*.tbmap    -text
*.tbchange -text
*.tbnames  -text
*.rd5      -text
*.pmtiles  -text
*.sig      -text

# The indexes ARE text and are meant to normalise — but they are also read
# byte-for-byte by tools that compare builds, so pin them to LF rather than
# leaving it to whatever each contributor's autocrlf happens to be.
*.json     text eol=lf
